From mark Fri May 16 23:08:11 1980
To: Lauren@UCLA-Security
Subject: network news

Does your news system have any particularly nice features that might
want to be included in the netnews program?  If you're worried about
the user interface, consider this:  one of the features of netnews
is that you can have it write a phony mailbox file for you (using
Bell conventions - you'd have to gimmick it slightly to put in 4
control A's on your system, probably) and then you can invoke your
favorite mail system on it.  The default interface happens to look
just like /bin/mail (on V7), but other mailers, such as msg, could
be used instead.  In particular, it might not be hard for you to
make up an interface program that looks to the user like the news
command you are running now.  (I don't know what your news system
looks like.)  I know this would be possible in our case, since we
use msgs right now.
	Mark

--- interested people
From RWK@MIT-MC  Fri May 16 02:57:18 1980
Date: 16 MAY 1980 0552-EDT
From: RWK at MIT-MC (Robert W. Kerns)
To: CSVAX.mark at BERKELEY, CSVAX.swd at BERKELEY
CC: ELLEN at MIT-MC

I'd be interested in functional specs on your news-net frob, as well as some
idea just what it gets used for.  The C software would be helpful for
implementing it on the VAX, as well, although it may be better to integrate it
into a mail system I'm writing.

You realize, of course, that BBOARD and :MSGS type messages are seen by a user
only once, and are not seen by him again unless he explicitly requests it.
Your note to ARPA-LIASONS implies this isn't true by comparing them with your
news-net.


From Zellich@OFFICE-1  Fri May 16 05:30:08 1980
Date: 16 May 1980 0525-PDT
From: Zellich at OFFICE-1
Subject: Re: a new network news program
To:   CSVAX.mark at BERKELEY
cc:   ZELLICH

In response to your message sent  16 May 1980 04:45:35-PDT

Thanks for the initial information on the news facilities.  I am
very definitely interested, from a couple of standpoints -- I would
like to supply the news to my inhouse users currently on the ARPANET
thru Tymshare's OFFICE-1 and OFFICE-2 hosts; and we are shortly to have
one or more UNIX systems inhouse, at least one of which will be an
ARPANET host.

Please add me to your mailing list for future information on the
news program.

Cheers,
Rich <ZELLICH at OFFICE-1>
